# worktree-layout

A way to lay out one project's checkouts so every branch gets its own worktree, a sandboxed agent in any of them can commit, and Herdr's sidebar groups the work the way you think about it. This skill holds the rules, and its script does the setup.

A project opts in with a line in its own `AGENTS.md` saying it uses this layout and to load this skill before touching worktrees there. The same file carries what this skill can't know about the project: where the primary was cloned from and its remote, how it names branches and work streams, and any setup a new clone or worktree needs, such as installing dependencies or hooks.

## Shape

```
<project>/                     plain folder, not a repo: the project's AGENTS.md
├── repo/                      the primary: a clone on the default branch
│   └── .worktrees/<branch>/   worktrees of the primary
└── <stream>/                  a work stream: a clone on that stream's active branch
    └── .worktrees/<branch>/   worktrees of the work stream
```

- **Every repo is an ordinary clone.** The primary, `repo/`, sits on the default branch. Sessions that work across the project, such as a driver, run there, so they have a checkout and load the repo's own skills along with the project's `AGENTS.md`.
- **A work stream is its own clone, not a worktree of the primary.** It holds one body of work that spans several branches, such as an epic and its stories, a milestone, or a feature built in parts, and its root sits on that stream's active branch. It is a local clone of the primary, so it hardlinks objects and takes almost no disk, with `origin` pointed at the same remote. Herdr clusters workspaces by repo, so a work stream's worktrees group under their own row only because it is its own repo. A single branch needs only a worktree of the primary.
- **Worktrees nest in their clone's `.worktrees/`,** each named for its branch, with `/` replaced by `-`. The clone's `.git/info/exclude` lists `/.worktrees/`, so they stay out of its `git status` without anything committed for it.
- **One level deep.** A work stream has worktrees, not work streams of its own.

## Names

The project chooses its names. The layout needs only that a worktree is named for its branch, and that a work stream folder is named for the work stream and is never `repo`. Herdr labels are free text; a short human name for the work reads best in the sidebar.

One scheme that works, for a project whose tracker links branches by a key prefix: a work stream folder is the lowercased key of the task that tracks the whole body of work alone (`abc-12`), and a branch is its task's key plus a short slug (`abc-34-settings-root`), with labels like `ABC-12 Settings` and `ABC-34 Settings root`. The tracker then links each branch to its task.

## Making a clone

`scripts/create-clone` makes the primary and every work stream; only the source differs. The primary clones from the remote's HTTPS URL or an existing checkout, and a work stream from the primary, which hardlinks objects.

```bash
scripts/create-clone --path <dir> --from <checkout|url> [--branch <name>] [--base <ref>] [--origin <url>] [--label <text>] [--setup <command>] [--no-workspace]
```

**It has to run outside the sandbox,** because Claude Code's sandbox write-protects `.git/config` and `.git/hooks` in every clone a session works in, so `git clone` and hook installation both fail there. A sandboxed agent never runs it: give the user the exact command, with the script's full path and every flag filled in, to run with `!` before it, and read its JSON line from their output. Pass the project's one-time clone setup, such as `--setup "make hooks"`, so it runs in the same unsandboxed step.

It prints one line of JSON, `{ path, branch, default_branch, origin, workspace_id, pane_id, created }`, and exits `0` on success, `2` when it needs judgment (`<dir>` is occupied by something other than a clone, or `--from` isn't a git repo; stdout is `{"status": "needs_judgment", "reason": ...}`), or `1` on a git, setup, or herdr failure, with diagnostics on stderr. `created` is `false` when it converged on an existing clone, and `workspace_id` and `pane_id` are `null` when no workspace was opened.

What it does:

1. Clones from `--from`, and points `origin` at `--origin`, defaulting to the source's own origin URL, or to the URL cloned. An SSH URL is rewritten as HTTPS, since the sandbox's network proxy carries no SSH; git then gets credentials from `gh auth setup-git`.
2. Fetches with prune, so `origin/*` holds the remote's branches rather than the source's local ones.
3. Checks out `--branch`, defaulting to origin's default branch. It starts from `origin/<branch>` when that exists, tracking it, and otherwise from `--base`, defaulting to `origin/<default>`, with no upstream. The local branch the clone copied from the source is deleted.
4. Sets `branch.autoSetupMerge=false` and `push.default=current`, and unsets `push.autoSetupRemote`. A sandboxed session can't write `.git/config`, and these keep everyday git from trying to: a branch cut from `origin/<default>` records no upstream, and a bare `git push` pushes to the same-named branch without one.
5. Adds `/.worktrees/` to `.git/info/exclude`.
6. Runs `--setup` in the clone.
7. Opens the clone as a Herdr parent workspace with `--label`, unless `--no-workspace` is passed or `HERDR_ENV` isn't `1`.

Re-running converges, and finishes a build that failed partway. Every step but the checkout is safe to repeat and always runs. The checkout runs only on a clone the script made and hasn't finished, tracked in its config as `create-clone.state`, so a re-run never moves or deletes branches in an established clone.

## Working in a clone

With the clone's config set as above, the git an agent needs works inside the sandbox, from the clone root and from a nested worktree: commit, fetch, merge, `git switch -c` from `origin/*`, `git worktree add` and `remove`, and a bare `git push`. `git branch -D` succeeds with a warning about the config lock. Anything that writes config fails, so never add `--set-upstream` or `-u` to a push, and don't set config from a session; when something truly needs a config change, it is the user's to make outside the sandbox.

## Herdr

Herdr's sidebar has one kind of object, the **workspace**. A **parent workspace** is on a repo's primary checkout, which here is the clone's root. A **linked-worktree workspace** is on a linked worktree. The sidebar clusters linked-worktree workspaces under their parent by repo; that cluster is display only and has no id.

- Open each clone, primary or work stream, as its parent workspace.
- Create worktrees from the parent workspace, with an explicit path in the clone's `.worktrees/` (`<clone>/.worktrees/<branch>`). Herdr refuses to create one from a linked-worktree workspace.
- A newly opened parent workspace has no repo metadata until its first worktree action. That's normal.
- Herdr lists a repo's worktrees in the order their workspaces opened. The `herdr-reorder-worktrees` skill re-sorts them without closing anything.
- A worktree made with plain `git worktree add` has no workspace until one is opened on its path.

## Sandbox

These are Claude Code sandbox behaviors the layout relies on or runs into.

- **A worktree can write its clone's git data.** A linked worktree's commits, refs, and index live in its clone's `.git/`, outside the worktree, and Claude Code's sandbox lets a session in a linked worktree write its main repo's `.git` on its own. A worktree needs no sandbox settings of its own to commit or fetch.
- **`.git/config` and `.git/hooks` are read-only** to every sandboxed session, in its own clone, in that clone's worktrees, and in any clone below its directory. That is why making a clone and installing hooks run outside the sandbox, and why the clone's config is set once at clone time.
- **Trust carries per repo.** Claude Code files a worktree's trust under its main repo, so only a clone itself and its first worktree show the "trust this folder" prompt.
- **A worktree that moves keeps absolute paths** in anything it built, such as a virtualenv, so rebuild those after `git worktree move`.

Codex's `workspace-write` sandbox makes every `.git` read-only, so git writes fail in a Codex session in either kind of checkout. This layout has no Codex handling yet.

## Teardown

Only when the user asks. Removal is fail-closed: refuse, and give the reason, when a worktree has uncommitted work, when a clone has local commits or branches that aren't on the remote, or when a live agent is running there. For a work stream, check every local branch in its clone, because those branches exist nowhere else until pushed. Removing a work stream closes its workspace and its worktrees' workspaces, then removes the folder.
